# Preview before large changes

Before a large change (new language feature, interface/security/compiler architecture, example rewrite, numeric/type-system change, or anything that touches more than one example or a public theorem family):

1. Show the owner a preview of the user-facing code: storage struct, one representative function body, one or two theorem statements as a Solidity developer would read them.
2. Wait for an explicit go. Do not spawn implementation/proof subagents until then.
3. Do not deep-dive internals, start proofs, or rewrite trees to figure out the design. Draft the surface, ask, then implement.

Exempt: build/lint, renames, docs, theorem-docstring quality, one-file bugfixes that do not change a public API or theorem statement.

Subagent briefs must say "preview already approved", or the subagent stops and returns a preview instead of editing.

Template — Amount redesign (what "show the code first" means):

```lean
structure Storage where
  reserve0 : Amount token0
  reserve1 : Amount token1
  -- …

def swap0for1 (amountIn : Amount token0) (minOut : Amount token1) : M (Amount token1) := do
  -- …

-- delta form: success ⇒ reserve product does not fall
Tx.run (swap0for1 dx minOut) ctx w = .ok (out, w') →
  w'.self.reserve0.raw * w'.self.reserve1.raw ≥
    w.self.reserve0.raw * w.self.reserve1.raw
```
